@subheading agent

@table @code
@item agent_cancel
Stop a subagent you spawned.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool agent cancel}
@item Surfaces: mcp, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: destructive
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{id} @tab string @tab yes @tab Task id returned by agent_spawn
@end multitable

@item agent_list
List the agent definitions available to spawn, and every task currently known with its state.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool agent list}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

Takes no arguments.

@item agent_models
List the models a provider offers, for use as agent_spawn's model argument. The value to pass is "provider/model".

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool agent models}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{provider} @tab string @tab yes @tab Provider name, from agent_providers
@end multitable

@item agent_providers
List the AI providers this cmacs can reach, and whether each looks usable. Use before agent_models when you want to pick a model for a spawn.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool agent providers}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

Takes no arguments.

@item agent_result
Collect what a subagent produced. Call it once agent_status reports `done'; on a `failed' task it returns whatever was produced before the failure, which is usually the explanation. For an agent you have sent more than one message to, this returns the answer to the most recent one. Pass `turn' to read an earlier one, or use agent_log to read the whole exchange.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool agent result}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{id} @tab string @tab yes @tab Task id returned by agent_spawn
@item @code{turn} @tab integer @tab no @tab Which turn's reply to read; omit for the latest
@end multitable

@item agent_spawn
Hand a piece of work to another agent, which runs in parallel with you. Returns a task id immediately -- it does NOT wait for the work to finish. Poll it with agent_status and collect the answer with agent_result once the state is `done'. Spawn several and poll them all rather than spawning one and waiting.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool agent spawn}
@item Surfaces: mcp, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: destructive, confirm=ask
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{task} @tab string @tab yes @tab What the subagent should do. Write it as a standalone instruction: the subagent does not see your conversation.
@item @code{agent} @tab string @tab no @tab Which agent definition to use; omit for the default
@item @code{model} @tab string @tab no @tab Model as provider/model, e.g. claude/claude-sonnet-4-6. Omit to use the agent's own -- call agent_models first if you want to choose one.
@item @code{title} @tab string @tab no @tab Short label for the dashboard
@end multitable

@item agent_status
Check on a subagent you spawned. States are draft, queued, starting, running, waiting-input, done, failed, cancelled and over-budget. Only `done' and `failed' are final.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool agent status}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{id} @tab string @tab yes @tab Task id returned by agent_spawn
@end multitable

@end table

@subheading ai

@table @code
@item ai_call
Send PROMPT to an AI provider and return the final answer. Like ai_prompt, but with 'tools':true the model is given the built-in agent tools (bash/read/write/edit/glob/grep/ls/web_fetch) and runs a multi-turn tool loop on a worker thread. Optional 'provider' / 'system' / 'model' behave as for ai_prompt.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai call}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 1800s)
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai call --prompt 'What is in ./README.org?' --tools}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{prompt} @tab string @tab yes @tab User prompt
@item @code{provider} @tab string @tab no @tab Provider name
@item @code{system} @tab string @tab no @tab System prompt
@item @code{model} @tab string @tab no @tab Model name
@item @code{tools} @tab boolean @tab no @tab Give the model the built-in agent tools
@end multitable

@item ai_embed_similarity
Cosine similarity of two texts' embeddings, in [-1, 1]: 1 is the same direction, 0 unrelated, -1 opposite. Use it to judge whether two passages mean the same thing -- near-duplicate detection, or checking an answer against a source. Needs a local embedding provider (ollama). Optional 'model' overrides the default embedding model. To SEARCH the notes corpus use memory_search instead; this compares two specific strings.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai embed-similarity}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 120s)
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai embed-similarity --a 'a cat' --b 'a kitten'}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{a} @tab string @tab yes @tab First text
@item @code{b} @tab string @tab yes @tab Second text
@item @code{model} @tab string @tab no @tab Embedding model
@end multitable

@item ai_list_providers
Return the list of supported cmacs-ai provider symbols.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai list-providers}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai list-providers}
@end itemize

Takes no arguments.

@item ai_lql
Run an LQL query on the libresearch server and return its rows (at most 100 shown). LQL pipes search results: search "glib", category: CODE | filter extra.stars > 100 | fields title, url -- or the shorthand gtk docs | filter site("docs.gtk.org"). 'explain' returns the plan without running it. An invalid query reports the server's position and caret excerpt.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai lql}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 120s)
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai lql --query 'gtk docs | filter site("docs.gtk.org")'}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{query} @tab string @tab yes @tab LQL query
@item @code{explain} @tab boolean @tab no @tab Plan without running
@end multitable

@item ai_open_chat
Open a cmacs-ai chat buffer. If PROMPT is given, the prompt is sent as the first user turn and streamed. Optional 'model' overrides the provider's default model. Returns the buffer name.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai open-chat}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai open-chat --provider claude}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{prompt} @tab string @tab no @tab Optional initial prompt
@item @code{provider} @tab string @tab no @tab Provider name
@item @code{model} @tab string @tab no @tab Model name
@end multitable

@item ai_prompt
Send PROMPT to an AI provider via cmacs-ai and return its answer. Optional 'provider' (claude / openai / gemini / grok / ollama / claude-code / opencode / claude-tmux / grok-build / antigravity / cursor) overrides the default; optional 'system' is a system prompt; optional 'model' overrides the provider's default model.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai prompt}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 600s)
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai prompt --prompt 'Name three GLib main-loop sources'}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{prompt} @tab string @tab yes @tab User prompt
@item @code{provider} @tab string @tab no @tab Provider name
@item @code{system} @tab string @tab no @tab System prompt
@item @code{model} @tab string @tab no @tab Model name
@end multitable

@item ai_research
Research a question on the web with libresearch and return a cited answer: the answer with numbered citations [n], then the sources (title, URL). Needs a libresearch server (cmacs-ai-libresearch-url). 'deep' lets the server run follow-up searches; 'pages' (1-20) is how many pages it reads. A run takes minutes.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai research}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 960s)
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai research --question 'What changed in wlroots 0.20?'}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{question} @tab string @tab yes @tab The question
@item @code{deep} @tab boolean @tab no @tab Run follow-up searches
@item @code{pages} @tab integer @tab no @tab Pages to read, 1-20
@end multitable

@item ai_web_fetch
Read a web page's text through libresearch (the server applies its own SSRF guard). 'archive' is never (default), fallback (use the Wayback Machine when the live page fails) or only; 'max_chars' (100-1000000) caps the text. Returns title, URL, the archived copy if one stood in, then the text.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai web-fetch}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 120s)
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai web-fetch --url https://docs.gtk.org/glib/}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{url} @tab string @tab yes @tab Page URL
@item @code{archive} @tab string @tab no @tab Wayback Machine policy One of: never, fallback, only.
@item @code{max_chars} @tab integer @tab no @tab Longest text
@end multitable

@item generate_image
Generate an image from a text prompt and insert it into a cmacs buffer. In an Org buffer the image is attached to the current node and previewed inline; elsewhere it is saved under cmacs-ai-image-dir. Optional 'references' is a comma-separated list of input images to condition on, each PATH or PATH::ROLE (e.g. "logo.png::style,cat.jpg::subject") -- roles tell the model what each reference is for. Optional 'provider' (gemini / openai / grok), 'model', 'aspect' (e.g. 16:9), 'size' (e.g. 1024x1024) and 'quality'. Optional 'buffer' names the target buffer; the current one is used otherwise. Generation takes tens of seconds.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool ai generate-image}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 300s)
@item Example: @code{emacsctl tool ai generate-image --prompt 'a brass orrery' --aspect 16:9}
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{prompt} @tab string @tab yes @tab What to depict
@item @code{references} @tab string @tab no @tab Comma-separated PATH or PATH::ROLE reference images
@item @code{provider} @tab string @tab no @tab gemini/openai/grok
@item @code{model} @tab string @tab no @tab Model id
@item @code{aspect} @tab string @tab no @tab Aspect ratio
@item @code{size} @tab string @tab no @tab Pixel size
@item @code{quality} @tab string @tab no @tab Quality level
@item @code{buffer} @tab string @tab no @tab Target buffer name
@end multitable

@end table

